# fresh_dev_wait_alias_delete_dies: DeviceCore::finish_wait clears its waiter's bit with a 32-bit mask (num::bit32, the shift taken mod 32), so deleting child 33 (opcode 49, DeviceCore::delete_fifo) clears bit 1 of the waiting word (waiting_stamps) while child 1 waits on a barrier, leaving child 1's waiting entry (its next index) set. Child 2, sharing stamp index 1 (nothing checks uniqueness), then waits on a barrier: DeviceCore::add_wait's mask test passes (bit 1 is clear), and its check that the waiter's entry is idle ends the process (DeviceDeath::WaiterBusy): the host dies. Child 1 and child 2 wait on child 3's first stamp, which never comes (a wait on the root would be released by the delete, itself a root command). Child 2 reaches its wait only after an 8 MiB NOP, so the death lands in the wait after its doorbell. Compare interplay_limits_child_wait_alias_dies, the same aliasing in DeviceCore::add_wait itself.
